## 1. Ops state (Redis)

Redis key `br:ops:mode`, JSON:

```
{ state: "live" | "degraded" | "down",
  reason: string,
  until: number | null,   // epoch ms; auto-lift
  setAt: number }
```

Read it in `proxy.ts` behind a **module-scope in-memory cache with a ~15s TTL** so warm
instances don't hit Redis on every request.

Resolution order:
1. If `process.env.OPS_FORCE_DOWN === "1"` → treat as `down`. (Break-glass; requires a
   redeploy but needs no network call.)
2. Read from Redis. **On any error, fail open** — treat as `live`.
3. If `until` is set and has passed, treat as `live` and lazily clear the key.

Audit log: Redis list `br:ops:log`, `LPUSH` + `LTRIM 0 99`. Every state change appends
`{ state, reason, until, at }`.

## 2. Dev auth (`lib/dev/auth.ts`)

- Env var `DEV_MODE_SECRET` (long random string).
- `POST /api/dev/unlock` — body `{ password }`. Compare against `DEV_MODE_SECRET` with
  `crypto.timingSafeEqual`. **Rate-limit this route using the existing per-IP limiter.**
  Never accept the secret via query string.
- On success, set cookie `br_dev`: value `${exp}.${base64url(HMAC_SHA256(exp, secret))}`,
  `httpOnly`, `secure`, `sameSite: "lax"`, `path: "/"`, 7-day expiry.
- Export `readDevSession()` — a single verification helper callable from `proxy.ts`,
  server components, and route handlers. Build it to be reusable; it will also gate the
  planned `/admin` dashboard.
- `POST /api/dev/lock` clears the cookie.
- `/dev` and `/api/dev/*` must be `noindex`, disallowed in `robots.txt`, and excluded
  from the sitemap.

## 3. `proxy.ts`

Matcher excludes: `_next/static`, `_next/image`, `favicon.ico`, `robots.txt`,
`sitemap.xml`, the web manifest, app icons, `/ph/` (PostHog proxy), `/api/health`,
`/api/dev/*`, `/api/cron/*`.

Logic when state is `down`:
1. Valid `br_dev` cookie → `NextResponse.next()`, plus a `x-dev-bypass: 1` request header
   so the app can render a "site is down for everyone else" banner.
2. Path starts with `/api/` **or** `Accept` includes `application/json` → return
   `503` JSON `{ error: "maintenance", reason, retryAfter }`.
3. Otherwise → return the maintenance HTML with `503`.

All maintenance responses carry `Retry-After: 900` and `Cache-Control: no-store`.

## 4. Maintenance page (`lib/ops/maintenance-page.ts`)

Export `renderMaintenanceHtml({ reason, until, brand })` returning a complete HTML string.

Hard requirement: **fully self-contained.** Inline CSS, inline SVG, no external fonts, no
JS, no network requests. It has to render correctly when the rest of the app is broken —
that is the entire point.

Design: calm and professional, not a joke 404 page. Include the reason text, an ETA when
`until` is set, a link to Eastside Audubon, and a contact email. Centre a single inline
SVG bird animation driven by CSS keyframes — a slow, unhurried wingbeat silhouette rather
than anything bouncy. Wrap the animation in `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` and
hold a static pose when reduced motion is requested.

Read the brand name from one exported constant (e.g. `lib/brand.ts`) — the app is being
renamed shortly and I don't want the name baked into this file.

Add `app/maintenance/route.ts` that calls the same function and returns `200`, **gated
behind the dev cookie**, so I can iterate on the design without taking the site down and
without exposing a public duplicate page.

## 5. Dev panel (`/dev`)

Server component, gated by `readDevSession()`. No cookie → render the password form.

**Site status.** Radio for `live` / `degraded` / `down`, reason textarea, an "auto-lift
after" select (30m / 2h / 6h / none), and a confirm step before writing. Default the
auto-lift to 2h — I should have to deliberately opt out of it, not deliberately opt in.

**Recent toggles.** Last 20 entries from `br:ops:log`.

**Debug overlays.** Store these in a second cookie `br_dev_flags` (JSON, readable by
client components). **Server-side, ignore `br_dev_flags` entirely unless `br_dev` verifies**
— otherwise anyone can set it.
- `cacheBypass` → sends `x-dev-nocache: 1`; the cache layer must honour it and skip both
  tiers.
- `showDebugBadges` → floating overlay showing, per request: cache tier hit/miss and age,
  remaining upstream eBird budget, remaining per-IP rate limit, and ORS circuit-breaker
  state.

**Degraded banner.** When state is `degraded`, render a site-wide banner carrying the
reason. Do not block the site.

## 6. Cron behaviour

Cron handlers read the ops state first. When `down`, skip **sending** push alerts but
continue ingest and caching, and still return `200` with `{ skipped: true }` so Vercel
doesn't record a failed job or retry.

## 7. Service worker audit

This is a PWA, so audit the service worker before finishing. If navigation requests are
served cache-first from a precached shell, returning visitors will never reach `proxy.ts`
and will never see maintenance mode. Navigations must be network-first (falling back to
cache only on genuine network failure), and the SW must not cache `503` responses.
Report what you find — don't silently rewrite the caching strategy.

## 8. Analytics

When `br_dev` verifies, call `posthog.opt_out_capturing()` by default, with a panel toggle
to re-enable capture. My own testing must not inflate the usage numbers.

## 9. Constraint — sensitive species

Developer Mode must **not** introduce any path that bypasses the fail-closed sensitive-species
location chokepoint. Debug output may log `suppressed: true` as a boolean; it must never
surface protected coordinates, regardless of dev session state.

